The last of the three proxy metrics from farmerbob-2jc is retired. Criterion::ScopeDiscipline
ranked on crates_touched -- "the task names one; more is scope creep" -- which penalised work the
task REQUIRED and said nothing about the rule the harness states and enforces. It now ranks on
scope_departures, from farmerbob_core::scope, the same measurement gate::judge already gates on.

All three arms scored 5 of 5 on the probe, with byte-identical rulings:

    0 vs 3 departures      Winner { arm: "a", on: ScopeDiscipline, margin: 3.0 }
    crates 1 vs 9          Undecided { tied: ["a","b"], next: [] }        -- the regression
    Some(0) vs None        Undecided { tied: ["a","b"], next: [ScopeDiscipline] }

That third line is the one worth keeping. An unmeasured scope does not win the criterion and does
not lose it; it is reported as a criterion that could not rank, which is exactly the treatment
Conformance and Clippy already give an absent value. The spec refused to let the arms invent a
rule here and told them to go read what the module already does. All three did.

THE CRITIQUE FILED THE SPEC'S OWN INSTRUCTION AS A DEFECT. codex-luna on glm-53-flash, both
claims: the `fb` crate will not build because adjudicate_cmd.rs constructs Evidence without the
new field, and the CLI never supplies scope_departures. Both true. Neither is a defect. The spec
says, in those words:

    The crates/fb build WILL break because callers construct Evidence without the new field;
    that is expected, it is not yours to fix, and reaching into another crate to silence it is
    the scope departure this very criterion measures.

Critics are given the task spec -- I have read the prompt files. And this same critic got the
identical situation RIGHT on scope-gate a few hours earlier, writing that the caller migration
"is therefore not a defect in this patch's scoped behavior". Same arm, same shape, opposite
reading, hours apart. Its critiques are useful often enough to keep, and inconsistent enough that
verifying each claim remains the only safe policy.

I MIGRATED THE CALLER, as the spec intended, and the comment there carries the rule: score.json's
scope_departures is absent -- None, not 0 -- when a worktree could not be assessed, and mapping
it to 0 would hand an arm a criterion it never earned.

Checked before blaming my own merge: `fb brief refusal-norm` prints "no candidate cleared the
gate", and it printed that before this change too. Pre-existing, not a regression.

